@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · LYRIS · STANDING" m="TKC: CR · TR; VOL. I · {v1:arrangement} · {v1:crownward2}"
# The record's version and the true one
rec:
On the record | Ceremonial (CR).
In fact | For thirty years she's run the domestic and diplomatic infrastructure of Imperial life (CR). She runs the Arrangement, inherited from the Anointed Wife who mentored her and refined into something close to a shadow government: about forty Anointed Wives who decide what should happen and arrange for their husbands to decide it (CR).
The law | She's the reason the Anointed Wife designation has the shape it has. She lobbied through Dorian for a decade and framed every gain as a gift he was giving (CR).
On camera | She doesn't appear on the Broadcast in any way that reveals anything, and she's spent thirty years making sure of it (CR).
On Eden | She'll recognize something she hasn't seen in thirty years of candidates, and won't decide right away whether that's a reason to help Eden or destroy her (CR). She's the blueprint Eden doesn't know she's following (CR).
prompt: The afternoon social where she sets the next week's policy | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · LYRIS · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 1 OF 2" m="TKC: CR · TL · WR; VOL. I · {v1:mountain} · {v1:arrangement} · {v1:teaches}"
# What she believes
rec:
Who taught her | Mountain, where the name of the dead is spoken to the mountain and not to God or the Temple (DOM). The Anointed Wife who mentored her (CR).
What she says in public | The Anointing was the most meaningful moment of her life. She's never said what actually happens in the room (CR).
What she believes | OPEN QUESTION: Whether she believes in God, the Temple or only the Arrangement.
Grief | The hour of private Binding of Return after the death of her own parents was the only time in Imperial Mourning she remembered what grief felt like (CR).
empire: What the Temple teaches about a woman like her
The husband leads, the wife supports, and the household reflects divine order in miniature (CR). INTERPRETATION: She's the Empire's model of that doctrine and the proof it's performance.
/end
small: OPEN QUESTION: Why did her own parents receive Imperial Mourning? Were they Vanes, or was she remembering them during someone else's (CR)?
prompt: What she prays, if she does, the night Azrael is declared dead | fill

@page rh="THE HOUSE OF VANE · LYRIS · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE · 2 OF 2" m="GEN 25.28; GEN 27; 1 KGS 1.11–31; 1 KGS 2.19; 1 SAM 19.11–17; PROV 31.23; ESTH 9.29"
# The text
rec:
Her name | *Lyris* has no biblical source. INTERPRETATION: It sounds like the lyre, the instrument David played to quiet a troubled king (1 Sam 16.23).
Rebekah · Genesis 25–27 | POSSIBLE: "Rebekah loved Jacob," and she engineers which son gets the blessing (25.28). **Where it breaks:** Rebekah steers the blessing to her favorite. Lyris's favorite was the elder, and the succession went to the younger anyway when the elder was declared dead (TL).
Bathsheba · 1 Kings 1–2 | POSSIBLE: The queen mother who works with a prophet to put her son on the throne, and is given a seat at the king's right hand. **Where it breaks:** Bathsheba's influence is on the record. Lyris's has been kept off it for thirty years (CR).
Michal at the window · 1 Samuel 19 | POSSIBLE: A wife who lets David down through a window and puts an idol in his bed to buy him time. **Where it breaks:** Michal saves her husband from her father. Lyris may have saved her son from his.
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her |
| Proverbs 31.23, "Her husband is known in the gates" | Esther 9.29, where the queen "wrote with all authority" |
box: Volume One
